# 6. Functional Requirements

Functional requirements describe **what the system must do**.

They represent capabilities visible to users, administrators, or other systems.

## Chat application examples

- A user can send a text message
- A user can receive a message
- A user can create a group chat
- A user can view delivery status
- A user can search previous messages
- A user can block another user

## E-commerce examples

- A user can search for products
- A user can add a product to a cart
- A user can place an order
- A user can make a payment
- A user can cancel an eligible order
- An administrator can update product inventory

## Vague statement vs actual requirement

```text
Vague:
Build a chat application.

Specific:
A registered user can send and receive
one-to-one text messages.
```

## Strong functional requirements are

- Specific
- Testable
- Clear
- Properly scoped
- Prioritized
- Connected to a user or system action

## User story format

```text
As a <user type>,
I want to ,
so that <business value>.
```

Example:

```text
As a registered user,
I want to follow another user,
so that I can see that user's posts in my timeline.
```

## Hidden functional requirements

The obvious feature is rarely the complete requirement.

Clarify:

- Can the item be edited?
- Can the item be deleted?
- Is deletion permanent or reversible?
- Can the action be canceled?
- Can failed operations be retried?
- Can users block one another?
- Does content expire?
- Are duplicate requests possible?
- What happens after payment failure?
- What happens after partial success?
- Are privacy settings required?
- Are administrator actions required?
- What happens when ownership changes?

## MVP prioritization

Separate requirements into:

### Must-have

Required for the first usable version.

### Nice-to-have

Useful but not essential for launch.

### Future feature

May be added after product validation or growth.

### Out of scope

Explicitly excluded from the current design.

This prevents over-engineering and keeps the design aligned with business needs.

---

# 7. Non-Functional Requirements

Non-functional requirements describe **how well the system must behave**.

A system can implement all features correctly and still fail because it is:

- Too slow
- Too fragile
- Too expensive
- Too insecure
- Difficult to scale
- Difficult to maintain
- Difficult to monitor
- Unable to recover

## 7.1 Performance

Performance requirements include:

- Response latency
- Processing time
- Upload time
- Download time
- Search response time

Example:

```text
99% of read requests should complete within 200 ms.
```

---

## 7.2 Throughput

Throughput represents the amount of work handled per unit of time.

Examples:

- Requests per second
- Queries per second
- Messages per second
- Transactions per second
- Events per second

Example:

```text
The system should support 20,000 requests per second at peak.
```

---

## 7.3 Concurrency

Concurrency represents simultaneous activity.

Examples:

- Concurrent users
- Concurrent connections
- Concurrent uploads
- Concurrent video streams
- Concurrent database transactions

---

## 7.4 Scalability

Scalability is the ability to handle increasing load.

The load may increase through:

- More users
- More requests
- More data
- More connections
- More regions
- More features

---

## 7.5 Availability

Availability represents how often the system remains operational and accessible.

Examples:

```text
99.9% monthly availability
99.99% monthly availability
```

Higher availability normally requires:

- More redundancy
- Better failover
- More monitoring
- More operational investment
- More cost

---

## 7.6 Reliability

Reliability represents the ability of the system to continue producing correct results, including when failures occur.

Reliability includes:

- Correct processing
- Data preservation
- Safe retries
- Duplicate handling
- Recovery
- Failure isolation

## Availability vs reliability

```text
Availability:
Can users access the system?

Reliability:
Does the system behave correctly and preserve data?
```

A service may be reachable but return incorrect results. In that case, it is available but not reliable.

---

## 7.7 Security

Security requirements can include:

- Authentication
- Authorization
- Encryption in transit
- Encryption at rest
- Audit logs
- Rate limiting
- Abuse protection
- Data privacy
- Secret management

---

## 7.8 Maintainability

Maintainability includes:

- Ease of changing the system
- Ease of debugging
- Clear component ownership
- Code testability
- Deployment safety
- Documentation
- Modular boundaries

---

## 7.9 Operational readiness

Operational requirements include:

- Logging
- Monitoring
- Alerting
- Distributed tracing
- Backups
- Disaster recovery
- Deployment automation
- Rollback support
- Health checks

---

## 7.10 Cost

Cost requirements can include:

- Infrastructure budget
- Storage budget
- Network budget
- Managed service cost
- Operational cost
- Engineering cost

---

## NFRs must be measurable

```text
Weak:
The API should be fast.

Strong:
99% of read requests should complete within 200 ms.
```

```text
Weak:
The system should be highly available.

Strong:
The service should provide 99.99% monthly availability.
```

```text
Weak:
The system should scale well.

Strong:
The system should support 100,000 concurrent users
and 50,000 requests per second at peak.
```

## Functional vs non-functional requirements

| Functional requirement | Non-functional requirement |
|---|---|
| A user can send a message | 99% of messages are delivered within the required latency |
| A user can upload a photo | The system supports the expected upload throughput |
| A user can search products | 99% of searches complete within the target latency |
| A user can create an order | Order data is preserved despite expected failures |
| A user can view a feed | The feed supports the expected peak read traffic |

