# 23. Myth vs Reality

| Myth | Reality |
|---|---|
| System design is only for major technology-company interviews | It matters for any growing product |
| Good code automatically creates good architecture | Correct code can exist inside a weak system |
| There is one correct architecture | Designs depend on context and trade-offs |
| Functional requirements are enough | Non-functional requirements often drive architecture |
| Estimation is random guessing | Explicit assumptions and rough math improve decisions |
| Seniority depends only on coding years | Architectural judgment is an important senior skill |
| Microservices are always more scalable | They introduce distributed-system complexity |
| More technology means a better design | The best design is the simplest one that satisfies requirements |
| Every system must support massive scale from day one | Architecture should follow realistic requirements |
| An architect never needs implementation detail | Architects decide where detail matters |

