# 24. Glossary

## Architecture

The high-level structure of a system, including components, interactions, and major decisions.

## Availability

The percentage of time a system remains operational and accessible.

## Back-of-the-envelope estimation

Fast, approximate calculation used to understand scale and guide decisions.

## Detailed design

A deeper view of critical workflows, APIs, schemas, storage, and trade-offs.

## Functional requirement

A statement describing what the system must do.

## High-level design

A bird’s-eye view of major components and data flow.

## Horizontal scaling

Adding more machines or instances to handle increasing load.

## Latency

The time taken for an operation or request to complete.

## Loose coupling

A design where components depend on clear contracts and have limited knowledge of one another.

## MVP

Minimum viable product, the smallest product version that provides core value.

## Non-functional requirement

A statement describing how well the system must behave.

## Peak traffic

The highest load the system must handle during a busy period.

## QPS

Queries or requests handled per second.

## Reliability

The ability of a system to continue producing correct results and preserve expected behavior despite failures.

## Scalability

The ability of a system to handle increasing load.

## Single point of failure

A component whose failure can stop the complete system or a critical workflow.

## System design

The process of structuring a system to satisfy business and technical needs under real-world conditions.

## Trade-off

A decision where improving one attribute can weaken another attribute.

## Vertical scaling

Increasing the capacity of one machine.

## Redundancy

Maintaining additional instances or copies to improve resilience.

## Replication

Maintaining multiple copies of data.

## Idempotency

The property where repeating the same operation produces the same intended result without unwanted duplicate effects.

## Throughput

The amount of work processed during a period of time.

## Concurrency

The number of simultaneous operations, users, or connections.

